  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nThere continues to be better model solution agreement with an upper \nlevel low deepening as it digs southeast from the Northern Plains \nthrough the Mid MS Valley today and then pivots thru the Ohio Valley \nThursday/Thursday night.\n\nDry and cool conditions continue today with an increase in clouds \nahead of this upcoming storm system. High temperatures today to \nrange from the mid 40s north to the lower 50s south.\n\nPrecipitation will spread up into ILN/s area late tonight and into \nThanksgiving day and then linger through Friday before tapering off \nFriday night. Pcpn should be be mainly rain but a mix with wet snow \nis possible late Thursday night into Friday morning as the upper \nsystem moves thru the area. A widespread 1 to 1.50 inch rainfall \nlooks likely from this system. \n\nSome light snow accumulations are possible mainly on grassy areas \nlate Thursday night into Friday morning but warm ground temperatures\nshould limit impacts. Will have to watch later runs and pcpn rates \nas this can overcome warm ground temperatures and lead to minor \nimpacts.\n\nThe pressure gradient increases with the approach of this deepening \nsystem leading to gusty winds. Expect northeast wind gusts of 35 to \n40 mph Thursday.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nSurface high pressure will offer dry conditions for Saturday with\nhighs from the mid 40s to the lower 50s. A progressive mid level \ntrough and surface low pressure system will track quickly thru the \narea bringing the next chance for rain Sunday. High pressure will \nonce again lead to dry weather for next Monday.",
